body:
Summary: This PR updates the Redwood quickstarts to include explicit, copy/paste-ready configuration examples for retries and timeouts across Python, TypeScript, and Go, using consistent naming and semantics per the SDK parity initiative. Motivation: Support tickets and customer escalations repeatedly show that developers rely on quickstarts as the “source of truth,” but the snippets previously either omitted retries/timeouts entirely or used language-specific defaults without explaining how to tune them. This contributed to “works in TS but not in Python” reports around 429 handling, Retry-After, and streaming disconnects. Scope: (1) Add a “Production defaults: retries + timeouts” block to each language quickstart. (2) Align option names and explanations across languages (overall timeout vs connect/read; max attempts; Retry-After handling; streaming caveats). (3) Add a small “Troubleshooting” note: always log request_id and SDK version; don’t retry streaming blindly without idempotency considerations. Non-goals: No SDK code changes here; this PR only changes quickstart content and templates. Changes (high level): - Added standardized config snippets to: python, typescript, go quickstarts (hosted API / chat + streaming variants). - Added short callout explaining retry policy: retries are attempted on 429 + selected 5xx + transient network errors; SDKs respect Retry-After when present; exponential backoff with jitter; max attempts cap. - Added short callout explaining timeout model: overall timeout is recommended; connect/read shown where supported; streaming timeouts are treated as “read/idle” timeouts and should be tuned to expected token cadence. - Added a “Debug fields” note showing how to capture request_id from responses/errors and include it in logs for support. - Updated “Configuration reference” links to point to the parity migration/standardization docs page (no new docs added here, just link targets used by the docs site). Example snippet (Python): ```py
from redwood import Redwood

client = Redwood(
    api_key=os.environ.get("REDWOOD_API_KEY"),
    timeout={
        "overall_ms": 60000,
        "connect_ms": 5000,
        "read_ms": 60000,
    },
    retry={
        "max_attempts": 4,
        "initial_backoff_ms": 250,
        "max_backoff_ms": 4000,
        "respect_retry_after": True,
    },
)
``` Example snippet (TypeScript): ```ts
import { Redwood } from "@redwood-ai/sdk";

const client = new Redwood({
  apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY,
  timeout: { overallMs: 60_000, connectMs: 5_000, readMs: 60_000 },
  retry: { maxAttempts: 4, initialBackoffMs: 250, maxBackoffMs: 4_000, respectRetryAfter: true },
});
``` Example snippet (Go): ```go
client := redwood.NewClient(redwood.Config{
  APIKey: os.Getenv("REDWOOD_API_KEY"),
  Timeout: redwood.Timeout{Overall: 60 * time.Second, Connect: 5 * time.Second, Read: 60 * time.Second},
  Retry: redwood.Retry{MaxAttempts: 4, InitialBackoff: 250 * time.Millisecond, MaxBackoff: 4 * time.Second, RespectRetryAfter: true},
})
``` Streaming note included in each language: - If the stream disconnects after partial output, surface partial text to the user and avoid automatic retry unless the request is idempotent (or your app can tolerate duplicates). - Prefer setting a higher read/idle timeout for streaming if you expect long generations; keep overall timeout aligned with user experience and server limits. Files changed (representative): - quickstarts/python/chat/README.md - quickstarts/python/streaming/README.md - quickstarts/typescript/chat/README.md - quickstarts/typescript/streaming/README.md - quickstarts/go/chat/README.md - quickstarts/go/streaming/README.md - shared/snippets/retry-timeout.{py,ts,go} - templates/quickstart-section-production-defaults.md Commits: 1) docs: add shared retry/timeout snippet templates 2) docs: update python quickstarts to include production defaults 3) docs: update typescript quickstarts to include production defaults 4) docs: update go quickstarts to include production defaults 5) docs: add troubleshooting note (request_id + streaming caveats) Review discussion (abridged): - Allison Grant: Requested we explicitly mention Retry-After behavior in the quickstarts (to reduce “retry storm” risk) and ensure the defaults shown match the approved standard (max_attempts=4, initial_backoff=250ms, max_backoff=4s). Updated copy and verified naming consistency. - Sabrina James: Asked to avoid implying that streaming is automatically retried; requested clearer language: “Do not retry streaming responses automatically; retry only at the application layer with idempotency guarantees.” Updated streaming sections accordingly and added a link to the idempotency example. - Dana Lo: Flagged casing drift in TS option names (overallMs vs overall_ms). We now show each language’s idiomatic casing but keep the same semantic fields; added a one-line mapping note under each snippet. - Mateusz Zielinski: Asked to ensure Go context cancellation is mentioned for streaming examples. Added a short note showing ctx.WithTimeout + cancel, and clarified that timeouts should be enforced via context where applicable. CI: - docs-lint (markdownlint + link-check): pass - quickstarts-smoke (build + run minimal compile checks for TS/Go snippets): pass - spellcheck: pass Merge: Squash merged into main. Follow-ups: - After this lands, we should update the docs site quickstart mirror to pull the new shared snippet blocks (tracked in ENG-5128). - Support macro update (INT-1199) should link to the new “Production defaults” section anchors.
